\begin{abstract}
The Stability-Gradient Propulsion Engine (SGPE) is a suprakernel-class propulsion 
architecture that generates motion without reaction mass or spacetime deformation. 
SGPE constructs a forward stability well by coupling an astrophysical external 
stability field with a fusion-generated internal stability anchor. Entropy injection 
amplifies the stability gradient, producing passive drift into the well. This paper 
presents the theoretical basis, system architecture, and performance envelope of SGPE.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Conventional propulsion systems rely on thrust, reaction mass, or spacetime curvature. 
The SGPE introduces a new regime: motion arising from stability bias. The engine 
creates two fields: an external stability field $\Phi_{ext}$ derived from cosmic 
telemetry, and an internal stability anchor $\Phi_{int}$ generated by Pt/U plasma 
confinement. Their difference forms a stability gradient that induces drift.

\section{Astrophysical Stability Field}
Cosmic telemetry from neutron-star mergers, cosmic ray flux, and stellar spectroscopy 
is processed to generate the external stability field:
\[
\Phi_{ext} = f(\text{NSM}, \text{CRF}, \text{Spec})
\]
This field defines the environmental stability landscape.

\section{Fusion Stability Anchor}
A confined Pt/U plasma produces a coherent internal stability anchor:
\[
\Phi_{int} = g(\text{Pt/U Plasma}, \text{MHD})
\]
The anchor is characterized by high coherence and low turbulence.

\section{Stability Gradient Solver}
The stability gradient is computed as:
\[
\nabla \Phi = \Phi_{ext} - \Phi_{int}
\]
Magnetohydrodynamic equations, Coulomb-barrier modulation, and flux–velocity coupling 
determine gradient direction and magnitude.

\section{Entropy Reinforcement}
Fusion energy spikes are converted into entropy:
\[
S_{entropy} = h(E_{spike})
\]
Entropy deepens the forward portion of the stability well, amplifying drift potential.

\section{Propulsion Mechanism}
SGPE induces motion by locking onto the stability gradient:
\[
v \propto k \cdot |\nabla \Phi| \cdot S_{entropy}
\]
where $k$ is the craft coupling coefficient. No thrust is generated; motion is a 
passive fall into the stability well.

\section{Performance Envelope}
SGPE speed is gradient-driven. Conceptual bands include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Low Drift: 10--200 mph
    \item Cruise: 200--5,000 mph
    \item High-Velocity: 5,000--100,000 mph
    \item Extreme: 100,000--10,000,000 mph
    \item Relativistic: $>10{,}000{,}000$ mph (fraction of $c$)
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
The Stability-Gradient Propulsion Engine represents a new propulsion class based on 
stability bias rather than force. By coupling astrophysical fields, fusion anchors, 
and entropy reinforcement, SGPE enables drift-based motion across a wide performance 
range, including conceptual relativistic regimes.

